---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, W } from '../lib/data';
---
<Base title="How We Research, Score and Rank Software" description="The exact formula behind every score on BestSoftwareGuides, how prices are collected and dated, and what 'verified' and 'reported' mean." path="/methodology/">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How we research, score and rank software</h1>
    <div class="answer"><p>Every score on {site.name} comes from a published formula applied to dated data. A vendor cannot pay to change a score. Where a ranking lists a featured partner first, it is labelled "Featured partner" and the highest-scoring tool is named separately; every other tool stays in score order. Prices are linked to the vendor's own pricing page and show the date they were last checked.</p></div>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>For each tool we record every plan, its monthly and annual price, free-plan limits, trial, minimum seats, platforms and a short list of category features. Each record stores the vendor's official pricing URL, the date it was checked, and any secondary sources used.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Verified</strong>: read directly from the vendor's pricing page on the date shown.</li>
      <li><strong>Reported</strong>: taken from recent published breakdowns of the vendor's pricing page (listed under Sources on every page), awaiting direct verification.</li>
      <li><strong>Not confirmed</strong>: we could not confirm a feature either way, so we do not claim it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Our pipeline re-checks vendor pages on a schedule. Any page whose data is older than 120 days is marked noindex until it is re-checked.</p>

    <h2>The score (out of 10)</h2>
    <p>The score is a <strong>value-and-coverage score</strong>: it rewards tools that confirm more of the features buyers ask about, cost less to start, offer free access and publish their prices. Score = 5 + 4.8 × the weighted sum below, so scores run from 5.0 to 9.8.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Weight</th><th>How it is calculated (0 to 1)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Feature coverage</td><td>{W.coverage * 100}%</td><td>Share of the category's key features the tool confirms.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Value</td><td>{W.value * 100}%</td><td>Entry paid price on annual billing: 1 for the cheapest tool in the category, 0.25 for the most expensive, 0.4 if no price is published.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free access</td><td>{W.free * 100}%</td><td>1 for a permanent free plan, 0.4 for a free trial only, 0 for neither.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pricing transparency</td><td>{W.transparency * 100}%</td><td>Share of plans with a published price or an explicit custom-quote tier.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Ties are broken alphabetically. Because the formula is fixed, rankings move only when the underlying data changes, and every change is visible in our <a href="/data/tools.json">open data file</a>.</p>

    <h2>Use-case rankings</h2>
    <p>Lists such as "Password Managers for Linux" apply one stated rule (for example "has a native Linux desktop app") to the same data as the full category ranking. The rule is printed on the page, tools that do not meet it are listed with the reason, and the order follows the overall score. A use-case list is only offered to search engines when at least three tools meet its rule.</p>

    <h2>User reviews</h2>
    <p>Tool pages can show reviews from LinkedIn-verified members. Their star average is shown next to our score and never changes it. Vendor and competitor staff reviews are labelled and left out of the average, and negative reviews are never removed for being negative. See the <a href="/review-guidelines/">review guidelines</a>.</p>

    <h2>What the score does not measure</h2>
    <p>The score does not measure how a tool feels to use or how good its AI output is. Where that matters we say so in the review text, and we keep it separate from the score.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>Vendors and readers can send corrections to <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. We update the record and its checked date once we confirm the change on the vendor's page.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
